# cppcheck suppressions. EVERY LINE NEEDS A REASON -- a suppression without one
# is a finding somebody hid rather than judged.
#
# Format: <id>  or  <id>:<file>  or  <id>:<file>:<line>
#
# 🔴 These were all verified individually against the source, not waved through
# because the job was red. The first run of the `analysis` job found no live
# defect; what follows is why each finding is not one.

# x_alloc(p, n) is `((p) = realloc((p), (n))) || (memerrexit(), 0)` (err.h:13),
# and memerrexit() is `die("out of memory")` -- which EXITS THE PROCESS. So on a
# failed realloc the original pointer is indeed lost, and it cannot matter: the
# program is already terminating. cppcheck is describing the shape of the macro
# correctly and the consequence wrongly.
#
# ⚠ If x_alloc is ever changed to RETURN on failure rather than die, this
# suppression becomes wrong and every one of these sites becomes a real leak.
# That is the one edit that must remove this line.
memleakOnRealloc

# series.c:698 declares `mapfileinfo key;` and sets only key.filename before
# passing &key to bsearch(). cppcheck cannot see through the function-pointer
# indirection to compare_mapfileinfo(), which reads ONLY ->filename
# (series.c:~660). The uninitialized members are never read.
uninitvar:series.c:698

# The engines' _assert macro is `((test) || (die(...), 0))`, and the
# "impossible state" assertions spell the test as `!"a message"` -- a deliberate
# idiom for "always fail, and put this text in the message". cppcheck reports
# the string-literal-to-bool conversion; that conversion is the point. The unit
# suite suppresses the matching -Wunused-value for the same macro.
incorrectStringBooleanError:mslogic.c
incorrectStringBooleanError:lxlogic.c

# messages.cpp:84 truncates an over-long message with
#     line = line.substr(0, maxMessageSize + 1);
# and cppcheck is right that this allocates a new string to assign a prefix of
# the same string back to itself. It is a real, tiny inefficiency, on a path
# that runs once per over-long message at load.
#
# 🔴 IT IS SUPPRESSED RATHER THAN "FIXED" BECAUSE THE SUGGESTED FIX IS WRONG.
# The message says "use resize() or pop_back() instead", and resize() is NOT
# equivalent here: substr(0, n) with n > size() returns the whole string, while
# resize(n) with n > size() PADS IT WITH NUL BYTES. maxMessageSize + 1 is
# routinely larger than the line, so the drop-in replacement would silently
# start appending nulls to most messages. The behavior-preserving form needs a
# length test first, and that is not worth a diff against upstream for one
# avoided allocation.
#
# ⚠ Introduced to the report by jc-57's C++ pass -- this file had never been
# analyzed before, which is the point of that pass existing.
uselessCallsSubstr:messages.cpp
